# THE DUTY CYCLE EVERY SUPPLY FILES, OR THE TYPED REASON IT FILES NONE. One row per
# nameplate: how much of a period the supply is actually available for.
#
# ⭐⭐ A SECOND AXIS OF DIVISIBILITY AND NOT A THIRD VALUE OF THE FIRST. A supply can arrive
#    in whole units AND be intermittent in time at once -- a truck that comes twice a week is
#    both -- so the amount question and the calendar question are separate columns.
#
# ⛔⛔ AND A BLANK WOULD MEAN THREE THINGS AT ONCE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS TYPED. ⚠️ ONLY TWO OF
#    THE THREE ARE ABSENCES. The first is a VALUE, and it spent two revisions filed as
#    `absent/reason = none`, which cost it both its size and its origin.
#      one whole period -- the supply runs continuously. A DUTY FRACTION OF ONE, filed as a
#                         value because that is what it is: `1 week` against a period of
#                         `week`, carrying an origin saying who could shorten it.
#      `notApplicable` -- the unit has no denominator, so "five days of what?" is malformed.
#      `unmeasured`    -- nobody asked.
#    Only the last is arithmetically dangerous, and only because a time slack cannot be
#    derived from a clearance when nobody knows whether the spare is spread evenly.
#
# ⛔ THIS RELATION DELIBERATELY DOES NOT REQUIRE A STATED NAMEPLATE AMOUNT. The window is a
#   statement about the calendar; a supply nobody has sized still has one.
-- pm:Nameplate/pm:Divisibility/pm:window, beside the amount unit that decides if it is answerable.
SELECT n.filing, n.layer,
       n.window_low, n.window_mode, n.window_high, n.window_unit, n.window_absent,
       n.amount_unit
FROM pm.nameplate n
